\section{Numerical accuracy}{

At the supplied ability, information and coefficients use
\code{a * (theta - b)} computed from the supplied inputs in double precision.
The law of T uses these logits with the identified discriminations described
below. In law-based computations,
estimates remain offsets from the midpoint of the difficulties, while
diagnostic zero searches use offsets from the supplied \code{theta}.
Accuracy depends on cancellation between items, each item's cumulant polynomial
near its zeros, the subtraction \code{theta - b} of nearly equal numbers,
and proximity to a coefficient's
zeros; a design average whose nonzero terms cancel to zero after rounding stops
with \verb{not resolved}, except in the zero search of \code{se_diagnostic()}, where an
unresolved average counts as a zero.
Log scales and normalised cumulant ratios reduce intermediate overflow and
underflow. A call can still stop with \code{representable} when an intermediate
value, such as a scaled cumulant ratio, is not representable, even if the
returned quantity would be. The four scaled risks (\code{smse_info}, \code{smse_boot},
\code{diff}, \code{smse_select}) are summed on the log scale: each term is formed as a
whole from the logarithm of its weight and of its two differences, so the sum
never forms a weight, a squared difference or a partial product on its own,
and none of them can stop a call or remove a term by leaving the double
range. Apart from requiring that the log weights and differences it receives
are finite, the sum checks the range only on the logarithm of the whole sum
and on the value rebuilt from it.
Weighted score terms are formed on the log scale, including the discrimination.
The function and its derivative use the same scale at each point for signs,
exact-zero tests and Newton steps, so the location of a root does not depend
on the double range, even when every score term is smaller than the smallest
positive double.
Inputs within the supported range can still yield a nonzero result computed
as outside the range of double precision, as in \code{delta2(1, 0, 300, "MLE")}, or
a nonzero logit whose \code{tanh(eta / 2)} is zero, as in
\code{delta2(1, 0, 2^-1074, "MLE")}.
These cases, and any non-finite logit, stop with \code{representable}.
Apart from these errors, the package does not return \code{Inf}, \code{NaN} or 0 in
place of a nonzero value, with five exceptions. The first is a probability
in \code{p_select} of \code{risk_diff()}, which can be returned as zero when it is
below the smallest positive double.
Discriminations whose ratios satisfy the lattice tolerance of eight relative
machine epsilons are identified with the commensurable design, even when
the supplied doubles are distinct. The identified design is used throughout
the law-based functions: totals, estimating equations, estimates, the law at
\code{theta} and at each estimate, and every report and rule coefficient at an
estimate. The \code{se_info}, \code{delta2} and \code{delta3} fields of \code{se_select()} still use
the supplied discriminations. Identified totals form one atom and can give
an exactly zero bootstrap SE; this is the second exception.
The third is a risk term whose report and target are computed as the same
double, which can happen where their exact values differ by far more than
the rounding of a double: its squared difference is zero. For example,
\code{risk_diff(c(1, 1 + 2^-36), c(0, 0), 0, "MLE")$smse_boot} can be zero even
though the mathematical risk is positive. A risk whose logarithm, as this
summation computes it, is below \code{log(2^-1074)} stops the call with
\code{representable} and the field name, even when ordinary arithmetic would round
the value up to \code{2^-1074}; a risk whose value rebuilt from that logarithm is
not finite, or is 0, stops the call in the same way. The value of a scaled
risk is
rebuilt from its logarithm. Rounding that logarithm alone contributes a relative
error of the order of 1e-13 where the logarithm is near 700 in absolute value;
errors already in the reports and log weights add to it, and so does rounding
the result to a double. A returned risk in the subnormal range is a multiple
of \code{2^-1074}, so rounding the result to a subnormal double can give a much
larger
relative error: in \code{risk_diff(rep(1000, 3), c(-1.44, 0, 1.44), -0.72 + 5e-9, "MLE")} the exact \code{smse_boot} is 1.6 times \code{2^-1074} and the call returned 2
times \code{2^-1074}. A
subnormal risk
is not claimed to be within \code{2^-1074} of the exact value, and no general
bound on the relative error of a returned risk is claimed.
Both decisions follow the logarithm computed from the reports, the target and
the log weights in double precision, not the exact risk, and the two can lie
on different sides of \code{2^-1074} or of the largest double. At \code{2^-1074} this
is not confined to risks close to the limit: when a report and the target
nearly
agree, their difference keeps few significant digits and the computed risk
can be wrong by a large factor. In \code{risk_diff(rep(1000, 3), c(-2 * r, 0, 2 * r), -r + 2^-52, "MLE")} with \code{r} set to \code{0.685794632511088} the report and
the
target agree to a relative 1.1e-13, the exact \code{smse_boot} for the supplied
numbers is 2.05 times \code{2^-1074}, which is representable, and the call
stopped. With \code{r} set to \code{0.6907256300571373} and the ability \code{-r + 2^-49} the exact value for the supplied numbers is 0.96 times \code{2^-1074},
which is below the smallest positive double, and the call returned
\code{2^-1074}. At the largest double, in \code{risk_diff(rep(512, 2), c(-B, B), 0, "MLE")} with \code{B} set to \code{1.4106628166864512}, the double that R gives for
\code{1042 * log(2) / 512}, the exact \code{smse_info} is above the largest double by
a relative 9.9e-15 and the call returned 1.7976931348622732e308. In general
the package does not claim that the outcome agrees with the side of either
limit on which the exact risk lies. The outcomes stated for the calls at the
abilities \code{-0.72 + 5e-9}, \code{-r + 2^-52} and \code{-r + 2^-49} and for the call
with \code{B} were measured with R 4.6.1 on aarch64 macOS and can differ on
another platform.
For \code{diff}, cancellation multiplies the errors of its terms by its condition
number (the sum of the absolute terms divided by the absolute sum). Nothing
said above about the limits of the double range or about subnormal values
bounds the relative error of a \code{diff} whose terms cancel. For \code{diff}, a term
is zero when either
factor in \code{w * (A - B) * (A + B - 2 * h)} is exactly zero. As a fourth exception,
when the positive and negative terms of \code{diff}, scaled by the largest absolute
term, cancel to exactly 0, the function returns 0. A \code{diff} of 0 means the two
risks are not distinguished in double precision, not that they are equal.
A nonzero \code{diff} whose logarithm of the absolute value, computed in the same
way, is below \code{log(2^-1074)} stops the call with \code{representable} and the
field name.
The fifth exception is a design average whose item terms are all computed as
0: the term of an item is formed from its cumulant polynomial (\verb{1 - 6u} for
\code{k4}, \verb{1 - 12u} for \code{k5}, and so on), and that polynomial can be computed as
exactly 0 where its value is not. With R 4.6.1 on aarch64 macOS,
\code{design_cumulants(1, 0, acosh(2))} returns \code{k4} = 0 and
\code{design_cumulants(1, 0, 2.2924316695611773)} returns \code{k5} = 0, although
their values at the computed logits are about -1.3e-17 and 2.3e-17.
